![kody code-review](https://img.shields.io/badge/kody-code--review-312B4B?labelColor=C9BBF2) ![Bug](https://img.shields.io/badge/Bug-B71C1C) ![high](https://img.shields.io/badge/severity_level-high-6B6B92)


**Fallback probe only tries the first balanced value after a fence, missing the answer**

When the first balanced value in the tail fails validation or never balances, the real answer later in the tail is never added, so `reroute-json` fails and the shard's findings are lost. Walk every balanced value, advancing past each one, instead of stopping at the first.



<details>
<summary>Prompt for LLM</summary>

````text
File libs/llm/structured-output-repair.ts, lines 495-500:

Fallback probe only tries the first balanced value after a fence, missing the answer

The after-fence probe adds only the FIRST balanced value found in the tail, so when that fragment parses but does not validate (prose such as `{a: 1}`, a `[ref]` in the text) — or when the first delimiter never balances, which makes `sliceBalancedJson` return null for the whole tail — the genuine bare answer later in that same tail is never added as a candidate and `reroute-json` still fails with `produced no valid object`, losing every finding of that shard (the #2255 symptom this PR targets). E.g. `'quoted:\n```ruby\n[ENV["KEY"]]\n```\nThe map is {a: 1}, here is my answer:\n{"suggestions":[...]}'` returns undefined. Walk every balanced value in the tail, advancing past each one, instead of stopping at the first.

Suggested code:

for (const block of [...fenced].reverse()) {
    let tail = answerText.slice((block.index ?? 0) + block[0].length);
    for (;;) {
        const after = sliceBalancedJson(tail);
        if (after == null) break;
        add(after.replace(/,(\s*[}\]])/g, '$1'));
        tail = tail.slice(tail.indexOf(after) + after.length);
    }
}
````

</details>


